            <ArticleTitle>Sleep apnea, sleep disturbance, and fasting glucose variability: a pilot study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>743-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Disturbed sleep and nocturnal altered breathing are related to disturbances of glucose metabolism. The present uncontrolled observational study explores the role of these factors on the variability of fasting glycemia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The number and duration of nocturnal awakenings and the fasting glycemia of 97 patients with type 2 diabetes treated with diet, metformin, or gliptins were recorded over seven consecutive days. During the same time period, the main respiratory indexes-oxygen disturbance index, apnea/hypopnea index, and respiratory disturbance index-were recorded for one night.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The three respiratory indexes and the number of nocturnal awakenings are highly correlated with the coefficient of variation of the fasting blood glucose recorded over the 7-day period at p &lt; .005 level. A multiple regression analysis showed that the variables in the model explained 86% of the variability.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Respiratory/sleep disturbances appear to be modulators superimposed on blood glucose levels determined by other factors.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2013 Diabetes Technology Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
